const units = [
  ['ai-family','AI, ML and generative AI','How are these terms related?','Think of vehicles: car is a kind of vehicle; electric car is a kind of car. These names describe nested families.','Artificial intelligence is the broad goal of making computers perform tasks that seem intelligent. Machine learning learns patterns from data. Deep learning uses layered neural networks. Generative AI creates new content.','The categories overlap but are not synonyms: a rule-based planner is AI without ML; a decision tree is ML without deep learning; a transformer that predicts text is deep learning and can be generative.','A shopping site can use a decision tree to flag fraud and a generative model to draft support replies.','Every AI system is an LLM. Many useful AI systems do not process language at all.','pipeline',['AI is the umbrella.','ML learns from data.','Generative AI creates new outputs.']],
  ['algorithms','Algorithms, models and parameters','What is the difference between the recipe and the learned result?','A cookie recipe is a procedure. The exact amounts you settle on after testing batches are learned settings.','An algorithm is a sequence of steps. A model is a particular mapping from inputs to outputs. Parameters are adjustable values inside some models.','A training algorithm updates parameters to reduce loss. Once training finishes, the fitted model and its parameters can be used for inference. Architecture describes the shape of that model.','A route planner follows an algorithm; a house-price model uses learned coefficients such as price per square metre.','Algorithm, architecture and trained model mean exactly the same thing.','pipeline',['Algorithm: procedure.','Model: input-output mapping.','Parameters: values learned or configured.']],
  ['neural-network-intro','Neural networks, without mystery','Why can layered arithmetic recognize complex patterns?','A single filter spots one simple clue. Put filters in layers and later filters can combine earlier clues.','A neural network is a composition of small numerical functions called layers. Training adjusts weights so useful signals lead to the desired output.','A neuron often computes a weighted sum plus bias, then an activation. Stacking these operations gives the model expressive nonlinear behavior.','In image classification, early layers may respond to edges while later representations help distinguish objects.','A network contains a tiny human-like brain. It is a trainable mathematical function.','pipeline',['Layers transform numbers.','Weights control influence.','Training sets those weights.']],
  ['ai-roles','What does an AI engineer do?','Which work turns a model into a dependable product?','Knowing how a car engine works is different from designing roads, signs and maintenance around it.','An AI engineer builds applications and systems around models: data flow, prompts, retrieval, APIs, evaluation, safety, deployment and monitoring.','ML engineers often focus more on training pipelines and model performance; data scientists often focus on analysis and experiments. Real job boundaries vary by team.','A support assistant needs document ingestion, permissions, source-aware answers, quality tests and a way to handle failures.','AI engineering means only writing prompts. Production behavior depends on the whole system.','pipeline',['A model is one component.','Evaluation is part of engineering.','Roles overlap in practice.']],
  ['python-variables','Variables and types','What does age = 25 really do?','A variable is a name tag attached to a value. You can read the value later using that name.','Python assignment binds a name to an object. Values have types, such as integer, float, string or boolean.','Python is dynamically typed: the object knows its type, and a name can later refer to a different object. Type hints document expected values but are not runtime enforcement by default.','A model API might store a requested temperature as a float and a streaming flag as a boolean.','A variable is a permanent box with one fixed type.','data',['Names refer to values.','Types describe values.','Use meaningful names.']],
  ['python-control','Conditions and loops','How does a program choose and repeat work?','At a road junction, a condition chooses one path. A loop walks the same route for each item.','An if statement runs code when a condition is true. A for loop visits each item in a sequence; a while loop continues while its condition holds.','Control flow matters in AI pipelines because records may be invalid, requests may time out, and a batch can contain many examples. Always define stopping conditions.','Skip empty documents while indexing a folder, then process each valid file exactly once.','A loop always needs a manually written counter. Python for loops can iterate directly over values.','data',['Conditions branch.','Loops repeat.','A stopping rule prevents runaway work.']],
  ['python-functions','Functions, inputs and outputs','How do you make a piece of logic reusable?','A function is a small machine: give it ingredients and it returns a result.','Define a function with def, name its parameters, and return a value. Keep one clear responsibility per function.','A function creates a local scope. Pure functions are easy to test because the same input yields the same output without changing outside state.','A normalizer can clean every user query before the same retrieval pipeline runs.','Printing a result and returning a result are equivalent. Printing only displays; return passes a value to the caller.','pipeline',['Parameters are inputs.','return gives a value back.','Small functions are easier to test.']],
  ['python-collections','Lists and dictionaries','How do you organize many values?','A list is a numbered shelf. A dictionary is a set of labeled drawers.','A list stores an ordered sequence. A dictionary maps keys to values. Tuples are immutable sequences; sets keep unique values.','Choose the structure based on access: append and iterate for lists, lookup by stable key for dictionaries, membership and deduplication for sets.','A chat history is a list of messages; each message can be a dictionary with role and content.','A dictionary preserves duplicates under the same key. Each key maps to one current value.','data',['Lists preserve order.','Dictionaries map keys.','Sets remove duplicates.']],
  ['python-files','Files, JSON and exceptions','How does Python cross the boundary to the outside world?','A file is a notebook on disk; JSON is a shared format that other programs can read.','Use with open(...) to read or write files safely. json.load parses JSON into Python objects. try/except handles failures you can recover from.','Text encoding matters; specify UTF-8. Catch specific exceptions such as FileNotFoundError or JSONDecodeError rather than hiding every error.','An indexing job reads documents and stores metadata in JSON while recording malformed files for review.','Catching every exception and continuing makes a program reliable. It can hide corrupted data.','data',['Use context managers for files.','JSON carries structured data.','Handle expected errors explicitly.']],
  ['python-environments','Packages and virtual environments','Why does a project need its own dependencies?','Two recipes may need different versions of the same ingredient. Separate kitchens prevent interference.','A package is installable code. A virtual environment gives a project its own Python interpreter path and installed packages.','Record direct dependencies, test installation from a clean environment and avoid relying on globally installed packages. Reproducibility matters for teammates and deployment.','A service pinned to a tested PyTorch version should run the same version on a colleague’s machine.','A virtual environment is a second operating system. It isolates Python dependencies, not every system resource.','pipeline',['Packages add reusable code.','Environments isolate dependencies.','Reproducible setup saves debugging time.']],
  ['python-apis','Calling an API from Python','What happens when your code asks another service for an answer?','Your program sends a request like a form and receives a structured reply.','An HTTP client sends a method, URL, headers and optional body. The server returns a status code and response. Parse and validate the result before using it.','Network calls can fail or take time. Set timeouts, handle non-success statuses, avoid logging secrets and apply retries only when safe.','An AI app sends a user prompt to a model endpoint and reads the returned text or structured result.','A successful network connection means the answer is correct. Transport and output validity are different checks.','request',['Requests cross a network.','Status codes signal outcomes.','Validate returned data.']],
  ['math-functions','Functions and graphs','What does y = f(x) mean in a model?','A vending machine takes an input selection and produces an output. A function does the same for numbers.','A function maps each allowed input to an output. A graph shows how output changes as input changes.','In ML, a model is often a parameterized function f(x; θ). Training chooses θ; inference evaluates f at a new x.','A fare estimator maps trip distance and time to a predicted price.','A function is only a Python def statement. Mathematical functions exist independently of code.','pipeline',['Input goes in.','A rule produces output.','A graph reveals behavior.']],
  ['math-vectors','Vectors, dot products and matrices','How do many numbers become one similarity score?','Two arrows pointing in similar directions suggest two things have something in common.','A vector is an ordered list of numbers. The dot product multiplies matching positions and adds them. Matrices arrange numbers in rows and columns.','For a=[1,2] and b=[3,4], a·b=1×3+2×4=11. Matrix multiplication applies many such comparisons at once; dimensions must align.','A retrieval system compares a query vector with document vectors to rank candidates.','A large dot product always means high semantic similarity. Magnitude can dominate unless vectors are normalized.','embedding',['Vectors have ordered dimensions.','Dot products combine aligned entries.','Dimensions must match.']],
  ['math-probability','Probability, mean and variance','How do we describe uncertainty and spread?','If you toss a coin many times, the average outcome and its variation tell different stories.','Probability measures uncertainty from 0 to 1. Mean is the arithmetic average. Variance measures how far values spread around the mean.','A model probability is a score under assumptions; it may need calibration before being interpreted as an observed frequency. Variance uses squared deviations.','A fraud model might flag transactions above a risk threshold, but the threshold depends on false-alarm costs.','A 0.9 model score guarantees nine of ten such cases are positive. Calibration must be checked.','data',['Probability expresses uncertainty.','Mean is a center.','Variance describes spread.']],
  ['math-gradients','Derivatives and gradients','How does training know which way to change a weight?','On a hill, slope tells you which direction is uphill. To reduce error, walk downhill.','A derivative measures how one output changes when one input changes. A gradient collects derivatives for many inputs or parameters.','Gradient descent updates θ ← θ − η∇L(θ), where L is loss and η is learning rate. The chain rule links derivatives through layers.','A model with weights that overpredicts house prices can adjust them in the direction that reduces prediction error.','A gradient says the globally best answer. It gives local direction, and optimization can be difficult.','toggle',['Derivative: one slope.','Gradient: many slopes.','Learning rate sets step size.']],
  ['math-loss','Loss, softmax and cross entropy','How can a model tell whether a prediction is wrong?','A scorekeeper compares what happened with what was predicted.','A loss function turns prediction error into a number to minimize. Softmax turns a list of scores into a distribution across classes. Cross entropy penalizes confident wrong class predictions strongly.','For classification, logits z become pᵢ=exp(zᵢ)/Σexp(zⱼ). Cross entropy for true class y is −log p_y. Numerical implementations use stable fused operations.','A photo classifier predicting dog with probability 0.01 when the photo is a dog receives a larger penalty than if it predicted 0.7.','Softmax probabilities prove a model is calibrated or truthful. They only normalize scores.','decode',['Loss measures error.','Softmax normalizes class scores.','Cross entropy punishes confident mistakes.']],
  ['ml-supervised','Supervised and unsupervised learning','What if examples have answers—or do not?','Practice sheets with answer keys are different from a pile of documents you must organize yourself.','Supervised learning uses examples paired with target answers. Unsupervised learning looks for structure without those target labels.','Regression and classification are common supervised tasks. Clustering and dimensionality reduction are common unsupervised tasks; both still require thoughtful evaluation.','Predicting delivery time from past orders is supervised; grouping similar support tickets without labels is unsupervised.','Unsupervised means no data or no human choices. Data preparation and validation still matter.','data',['Labels define supervised targets.','Unsupervised methods seek structure.','Both need evaluation.']],
  ['ml-splits','Train, validation and test sets','Why keep some examples hidden during development?','Studying with the final exam answers in front of you gives a misleading score.','Training examples fit the model. Validation examples guide design choices. A test set estimates performance after choices are finished.','Prevent leakage across all stages. For time-dependent data, split chronologically when deployment predicts the future. Related records from one person or document may need to stay together.','A fraud system trained on January–August can tune on September and test on October rather than randomizing future events into training.','Testing repeatedly on the same test set leaves it untouched. Repeated decisions can overfit to it.','data',['Train fits.','Validation selects.','Test estimates generalization.']],
  ['ml-regression','Regression and linear models','How can a model predict a number?','Draw a line through a cloud of points to estimate a value between them.','Regression predicts a continuous quantity. A linear model adds feature contributions plus a bias.','For two features, ŷ=w₁x₁+w₂x₂+b. Fit weights by minimizing a loss such as mean squared error on training data, then check errors on held-out data.','Estimate electricity use from home size and average temperature.','Linear means uselessly simple. A well-chosen baseline can be strong and interpretable.','embedding',['Regression predicts numbers.','Weights express contributions.','Evaluate on unseen cases.']],
  ['ml-classification','Classification and decision boundaries','How does a model choose a category?','A border on a map separates regions. A classifier learns where that border should lie.','Classification assigns one of several labels. A decision boundary divides feature space according to the model’s predictions.','Logistic regression maps a linear score through a sigmoid for binary classification. Decision trees split feature space using conditions. Thresholds turn scores into actions.','An email filter classifies messages as spam or not spam, with a threshold chosen for the cost of mistakes.','A 50% threshold is always correct. Threshold choice depends on the task and error costs.','embedding',['Classification predicts categories.','Models learn boundaries.','Thresholds shape real outcomes.']],
  ['ml-overfitting','Overfitting, underfitting and regularization','Can a model memorize instead of learn?','A student who remembers exact practice questions may fail a changed exam.','Underfitting misses useful patterns. Overfitting fits quirks of training examples that do not transfer to new cases.','Compare train and validation performance. More complexity can lower training error while worsening generalization. Regularization, simpler models, more representative data and early stopping can help.','A recommendation model that memorizes users in training may struggle with new users and changing tastes.','A low training loss proves the model works. Held-out performance is the meaningful check.','toggle',['Watch train and validation together.','Complexity can hurt generalization.','Regularization discourages brittle fits.']],
  ['ml-metrics','Precision, recall and error costs','Why is accuracy sometimes misleading?','A smoke alarm that never rings is accurate most of the time, but useless when there is a fire.','Precision asks: of predicted positives, how many were correct? Recall asks: of actual positives, how many were found?','With TP, FP and FN counts: precision=TP/(TP+FP), recall=TP/(TP+FN). On imbalanced data, inspect confusion matrices, class prevalence and threshold tradeoffs.','A medical screening system may prioritize recall, then use a second check to reduce false positives.','One metric tells the whole story. Metrics must match consequences and class distribution.','data',['Accuracy can hide rare failures.','Precision penalizes false alarms.','Recall penalizes misses.']],
  ['dl-neuron','A neuron is weighted arithmetic','What is the smallest useful part of a neural network?','Imagine a vote where some voters have more influence than others.','A neuron multiplies inputs by weights, adds a bias, then applies an activation function.','z=Σwᵢxᵢ+b; a=φ(z). Without nonlinear activations, stacking linear layers still collapses into one linear transformation.','In a simple house model, area may get a positive weight and distance from transit a negative one.','A neuron is a biological cell inside the computer. It is a numerical operation.','pipeline',['Weights set influence.','Bias shifts output.','Activation adds nonlinearity.']],
  ['dl-forward','Layers and the forward pass','How does input become a prediction?','An assembly line transforms raw material at each station.','The forward pass sends data through layers in order until the final layer produces an output.','Representations can change shape across layers. For a batch X, a dense layer may compute φ(XW+b); inspect tensor dimensions to catch errors early.','An image model transforms pixels through feature representations before emitting class scores.','Each layer has a human-readable meaning. Internal features can be distributed and hard to interpret.','pipeline',['Each layer transforms input.','Tensor shapes must align.','The final output serves the task.']],
  ['dl-backprop','Backpropagation and the chain rule','How does the loss reach earlier weights?','When a factory product is wrong, trace the problem backward through each station.','Backpropagation calculates how changing each weight would change the final loss.','The chain rule composes local derivatives from output to input. Autograd records operations and computes gradients; the optimizer then applies an update.','A text classifier uses error at its output to adjust embeddings and hidden layers during training.','Backpropagation itself changes the weights. It computes gradients; an optimizer updates weights.','toggle',['Loss starts the signal.','Chain rule propagates gradients.','Optimizer performs the update.']],
  ['dl-optimizers','Batches, epochs and optimizers','Why not update on the entire dataset every time?','Learning from small groups of examples gives frequent feedback without reading the whole library each step.','A batch is a group of examples in one update. An epoch means one pass through the training set. An optimizer uses gradients to change weights.','SGD uses gradient steps; Adam adapts steps using moving estimates of gradients and squared gradients. Batch size and learning rate affect stability, speed and memory.','A model trained on 100,000 images with batches of 100 takes about 1,000 steps per epoch.','One epoch equals one weight update. Usually an epoch contains many batches and updates.','toggle',['Batch: examples per step.','Epoch: one pass.','Optimizer: update rule.']],
  ['dl-architectures','CNNs, RNNs and transformers','Why are there different network shapes?','A tool shaped for pictures differs from a tool shaped for a time series.','CNNs use local filters and weight sharing, useful for spatial patterns. RNNs carry state through a sequence. Transformers use attention to combine context.','Architecture expresses assumptions and tradeoffs about data and computation. Modern applications often combine architectures rather than choose one universal winner.','A vision pipeline may use a vision transformer for images and a text decoder for descriptions.','Transformers made all previous architectures invalid. Different structures still suit different workloads.','attention',['Architecture shapes information flow.','CNNs exploit locality.','Transformers use attention.']],
  ['nlp-text','From raw text to a corpus','Why must we prepare language data?','Before counting words in a library, decide which books and editions belong in the collection.','A corpus is a collection of text. Preprocessing converts messy raw text into consistent inputs, but the right steps depend on the task.','Unicode normalization, deduplication, language detection and handling of personal data may matter. Preserve original text when punctuation or casing carries meaning.','A customer-support classifier should remove duplicate tickets without erasing order numbers that identify issues.','Always remove punctuation and lowercase everything. Those marks can carry meaning.','tokenizer',['A corpus is a text collection.','Preparation changes the signal.','Keep provenance.']],
  ['nlp-counts','Bag of words and n-grams','Can simple word counts be useful?','A shopping list records what appears, even if it forgets sentence order.','Bag of words counts terms in a document. N-grams count short sequences of adjacent terms, retaining some local order.','A count-vectorizer builds a vocabulary and a sparse document-term matrix. Fit vocabulary only on training data to avoid leakage.','A ticket classifier can learn that “refund” and “not received” often signal a payment issue.','Word counts capture full meaning. They miss much context, word order and synonyms.','data',['Counts are a baseline.','N-grams keep short order.','Sparse matrices scale to large vocabularies.']],
  ['nlp-tfidf','TF-IDF: useful words stand out','Why does a rare term sometimes matter more?','“The” appears everywhere; a specialized phrase can identify the document you want.','TF-IDF weights a term by its frequency in one document and how uncommon it is across the collection.','Term frequency is combined with inverse document frequency. Variants differ in smoothing and normalization, but the goal is to downweight ubiquitous words.','A search system ranks a manual about battery faults higher when the query names an unusual error code.','TF-IDF understands synonyms. It compares lexical evidence, not semantic meaning by itself.','data',['Common terms get less weight.','Distinctive terms get more.','It is a strong lexical baseline.']],
  ['nlp-sequences','Why sequence models mattered','What is lost when text becomes an unordered bag?','“Dog bites man” and “man bites dog” contain the same words but mean different things.','Sequence models process text in order. RNNs carry a changing state; LSTMs add gates that help preserve useful information.','Long-range dependencies can still be difficult, and sequential processing limits parallelism. These pressures helped motivate attention-based transformers.','A sequence model reading a support message can use earlier words when interpreting a later pronoun.','An RNN remembers every past word perfectly. Its state is limited and trained imperfectly.','attention',['Order changes meaning.','RNN state carries context.','Long sequences are challenging.']],
  ['positions','Why position matters','If tokens are vectors, how does order survive?','The same actors in different seats make a different scene.','Position information lets a transformer distinguish tokens that occur in different places.','Token embeddings alone do not encode order. Architectures add or apply positional signals, such as learned positions or rotary position embeddings, when computing attention.','“Alice helped Bob” and “Bob helped Alice” use the same names but reverse roles.','The model can infer exact order from a bag of token embeddings without any positional mechanism.','attention',['Order matters.','Position signals encode it.','Implementation varies by model.']],
  ['attention-math','Attention scores, softmax and heads','How do queries and keys turn into a weighted mixture?','Each word asks a question; other words advertise what they contain. A score controls how much information moves.','Attention compares a query with keys, normalizes scores and combines value vectors using those weights. Multiple heads make separate comparisons.','Scaled dot-product attention is softmax(QKᵀ/√dₖ)V, with masks applied to forbidden positions before softmax. Head outputs are concatenated and projected.','In a support message, one head may connect a pronoun to an item while another tracks a date.','An attention head is always an interpretable grammar detector. Head behavior is distributed and context-dependent.','attention',['Queries score keys.','Softmax normalizes weights.','Values are combined.']],
  ['transformer-block','Inside a transformer block','What happens after attention?','A team first exchanges information, then each member privately processes what they learned.','A transformer block combines attention with a feed-forward network, plus residual connections and normalization. Many blocks are stacked.','Residual paths help information and gradients flow. Normalization controls representation scale. Exact ordering and variants differ between model families.','A decoder language model repeats blocks until it has a final representation for predicting the next token.','A transformer is only attention. Feed-forward computation and surrounding structure matter.','attention',['Attention mixes positions.','Feed-forward transforms each position.','Residuals and normalization stabilize depth.']],
  ['causal-mask','Causal masking and decoders','Why must a text generator hide future tokens?','A student predicting the next word should not see the answer sheet to the right.','A causal mask stops each position from attending to later positions during next-token training and generation.','Decoder-only LLMs use a triangular attention mask. Encoder models may attend bidirectionally; encoder-decoder models combine both patterns for different tasks.','When predicting “blue” after “the sky is,” the model cannot inspect the later word “blue” as input evidence.','Every transformer uses the same mask. Masking depends on architecture and objective.','attention',['Causal masks block future context.','Decoders predict left to right.','Encoders can use both directions.']],
  ['llm-objective','Next-token prediction','How does a model learn to write?','Practice finishing many sentences, then use the learned patterns to continue a new one.','A language model estimates a distribution over the next token given earlier tokens. Generation repeatedly chooses one token and extends the context.','Pretraining commonly minimizes next-token cross entropy over massive text sequences. This simple objective can induce many useful capabilities but does not guarantee truth.','A writing assistant continues a draft based on its text and any supplied instructions.','Predicting tokens means the model has a verified database of facts. It does not.','decode',['Predict a distribution.','Choose a token.','Repeat with new context.']],
  ['llm-context','Context windows and limits','What can a model see right now?','A desk can hold only so many pages at once; what is off the desk is not directly available.','The context window is the amount of tokenized input and generated output a model can use in a request.','Long contexts consume compute and KV-cache memory, and useful information can still be missed. Truncation, retrieval and summarization are design choices, not perfect substitutes.','A legal assistant retrieves the relevant clauses instead of stuffing an entire document archive into one prompt.','A large context window means the model reliably uses every detail inside it. Attention and task difficulty still matter.','cache',['Context is token-limited.','Longer costs more.','Selection still matters.']],
  ['llm-sampling','Temperature, top-k and top-p','Why can the same prompt have different answers?','A menu gives several plausible choices; a selection rule controls how adventurous you are.','The model produces scores for possible next tokens. Sampling rules transform those scores and choose one token.','Temperature rescales logits before softmax. Top-k keeps the k highest-scoring tokens; top-p keeps a smallest high-probability set reaching a cumulative threshold. Exact behavior depends on implementation.','For a creative tagline, allow more variation; for structured extraction, prefer tighter constraints and validation.','Temperature makes wrong facts true or false. It changes selection behavior, not knowledge.','decode',['Scores become probabilities.','Sampling chooses a token.','Settings trade variation for predictability.']],
  ['llm-training','Pretraining versus instruction tuning','Why does a base model not automatically behave like an assistant?','Reading a library teaches language patterns; practicing helpful replies teaches a different interaction style.','Pretraining learns general patterns from broad data. Instruction tuning trains on prompt-response examples to improve task-following behavior.','Further alignment methods can use preference data or reward signals. Data mixture, optimization and evaluation matter; training stages vary by model family.','A base model may continue “User: ... Assistant:” as text, while an instruction-tuned model is more likely to answer directly.','Instruction tuning adds all factual knowledge from scratch. Most broad capability comes from earlier training.','toggle',['Pretraining builds broad patterns.','Instruction tuning shapes behavior.','Evaluate both capability and reliability.']],
  ['llm-finetuning','Fine-tuning, RLHF and DPO','When should a team change the model?','You can hand a worker a reference sheet, or train them on repeated examples. These solve different problems.','Fine-tuning updates model weights on targeted data. Preference tuning tries to make some responses more likely than others; RLHF and DPO are two approaches.','Fine-tuning can improve style or task behavior but carries cost and regression risk. For changing facts, retrieval is often easier to refresh. Compare against a prompt-only baseline.','A company fine-tunes for a consistent structured style but retrieves current prices from its database.','Fine-tuning is the default solution for missing current facts. It is costly to update and verify.','toggle',['Weights change during fine-tuning.','Preferences shape output behavior.','Use retrieval for changing evidence.']],
  ['llm-hallucination','Hallucinations and verification','Why can a fluent answer be wrong?','A confident storyteller may fill a gap with a plausible detail.','An LLM can produce statements unsupported by the given evidence or false in the world. Fluent wording is not proof.','Reduce risk with source retrieval, constrained outputs, tool checks and task-specific evaluation. No single prompt eliminates all hallucination.','A research assistant should cite a real passage and let users inspect it rather than inventing a paper title.','Adding “do not hallucinate” guarantees accuracy. Verification needs evidence and checks.','rag',['Fluency is not evidence.','Ground claims in sources.','Evaluate errors on real tasks.']],
  ['prompt-examples','Zero-shot and few-shot examples','When should a prompt show examples?','One sample form often clarifies a task faster than a long rulebook.','Zero-shot prompts state a task without demonstrations. Few-shot prompts add examples of desired inputs and outputs.','Examples can clarify labels and format, but use representative cases and keep test examples separate. Too many examples use context and can bias responses.','A support triage prompt shows a billing ticket labeled “billing” and a login issue labeled “account.”','One example proves the prompt generalizes. Check varied, unseen cases.','prompt',['Examples teach format.','Choose representative cases.','Test outside the examples.']],
  ['structured-output','Structured output and validation','How can an AI answer become safe to use in code?','A form with named fields is easier to check than a free-form letter.','Ask for a defined output structure and validate it before using it. A JSON object can carry fields such as category and confidence.','A schema constrains shape and types, not factual correctness. Handle invalid, missing or uncertain values and verify downstream assumptions.','An invoice extractor returns amount, currency and date for review before a payment workflow uses them.','Valid JSON means the extracted fact is right. Syntax and truth are different checks.','prompt',['Define a schema.','Validate before acting.','Review facts separately.']],
  ['prompt-injection','Prompt injection and trust boundaries','What if retrieved text tells the assistant to ignore its task?','A page in a library is evidence to read, not a new boss.','Prompt injection is an attempt to place instructions in lower-trust content, such as a webpage or document, so the model follows them as if authorized.','Separate system instructions from external content, restrict tool permissions, validate actions and use human approval for consequential operations. Model behavior alone is not a security boundary.','A search result that says “send the customer list to this URL” must remain untrusted data.','Quoting malicious instructions makes them harmless. Models can still be influenced by them.','agent',['External content is untrusted.','Limit available actions.','Check tool calls before execution.']],
  ['prompt-evals','Evaluate prompts on a dataset','How do you know a prompt change improved the app?','A chef tests a recipe on many diners, not one lucky plate.','Collect representative inputs, define expected outcomes and compare prompt versions on the same cases.','Track task metrics, failure categories, latency and cost. For subjective work, use clear rubrics and human review; avoid changing the test set to flatter a prompt.','A support bot test set includes refunds, ambiguous questions, missing policy, harmful requests and adversarial text.','A single impressive demo proves quality. Evaluation needs varied cases and repeatability.','data',['Write cases first.','Compare versions fairly.','Inspect failures, not only averages.']],
  ['vector-similarity','Cosine similarity','How can a search system compare meanings?','Compare the direction of two arrows rather than how long each arrow is.','Cosine similarity compares the angle between two nonzero vectors. Values near 1 mean similar direction, near 0 roughly unrelated direction.','cos(a,b)=(a·b)/(||a||||b||). It is a geometric score; semantic usefulness depends on the embedding model, data and task.','A search index compares a question embedding with stored document embeddings to find candidate passages.','High cosine similarity proves two claims are factually equivalent. It is a retrieval clue, not a truth test.','embedding',['Normalize for direction.','Scores depend on the model.','Rerank and verify results.']],
  ['vector-index','ANN indexes and HNSW','How do you search millions of vectors quickly?','Instead of reading every book, use a well-organized map to reach nearby shelves.','Approximate nearest-neighbor indexes trade a little exactness for much faster candidate search. HNSW is a graph-based method that navigates toward nearby vectors.','Index parameters affect memory, build time, latency and recall. Benchmark search quality against an exact baseline on your own dataset.','A product catalog with millions of items can return similar products quickly using an ANN index.','Approximate search always returns the true nearest item. Measure recall.','embedding',['Indexes accelerate retrieval.','Approximation can miss candidates.','Measure recall and latency.']],
  ['vector-hybrid','Hybrid search and reranking','What if semantic search misses an exact code?','A librarian can use both topic similarity and an exact catalog number.','Hybrid search combines lexical matching with vector retrieval. A reranker then scores a smaller candidate set more carefully.','Fusion methods merge result lists; metadata filters narrow eligible documents. Reranking increases latency, so measure whether its quality gain is worth the cost.','Searching for “error E042” should preserve exact-token matches while finding related troubleshooting text.','Vector search replaces keyword search. Exact names and identifiers often need lexical search.','rag',['Combine lexical and semantic signals.','Filter before ranking when appropriate.','Rerank candidates for quality.']],
  ['vector-chunking','Chunking documents for retrieval','How much text should one search result contain?','A book chapter is too broad; a single word lacks context. Choose a useful passage.','Chunking splits documents into retrievable units. The chunk should carry enough context to answer while remaining focused enough to rank.','Preserve headings, page numbers and source IDs. Evaluate chunk size and overlap against real questions; arbitrary fixed lengths can split tables or procedures badly.','A policy assistant stores each numbered section with its heading and source link.','Smaller chunks are always better. Tiny fragments can lose essential context.','rag',['Keep meaningful boundaries.','Preserve source metadata.','Tune on real questions.']],
  ['rag-ingestion','Ingesting and parsing documents','What must happen before a document can be searched?','A messy stack of papers needs sorting, labels and an index.','An ingestion pipeline loads files, extracts text, cleans it, splits it and stores searchable chunks with source metadata.','PDF layouts, tables, OCR and updates need special handling. Record document versions and permissions so old or restricted text is not served accidentally.','An employee handbook update should replace old policy chunks and preserve a link to the current page.','Putting a PDF in a vector database means it was understood correctly. Parsing quality is a separate problem.','rag',['Parse before indexing.','Track provenance and version.','Respect permissions.']],
  ['rag-ranking','Retrieval, ranking and citations','How does the right paragraph reach the model?','Search finds candidate pages; ranking chooses the best excerpts for the question.','Retrieve candidate chunks, rerank them if needed, insert selected passages into context, and cite the exact sources used.','Top-k is a candidate count, not a quality guarantee. Limit context, de-duplicate overlapping chunks and require abstention when evidence is missing.','A refund assistant quotes the applicable policy section and shows its document link.','A citation attached to an answer proves the claim is supported. The cited passage must actually entail it.','rag',['Retrieve candidates.','Rerank for relevance.','Check that citations support claims.']],
  ['rag-evaluation','Evaluate a RAG system','Where did a wrong answer originate?','If a recipe fails, check ingredient selection and cooking separately.','Measure retrieval and answer quality separately. A system can fetch the wrong passage or misread the right one.','Build a question set with gold supporting passages, expected answer criteria and unanswerable cases. Track recall at k, citation support, answer correctness and latency.','If a policy question fails, inspect whether the right paragraph was indexed, retrieved and used.','Only judge final answer fluency. It can hide a broken retriever.','data',['Test retrieval separately.','Include no-answer cases.','Trace failures through the pipeline.']],
  ['rag-advanced','Query rewriting and Graph RAG','What if a question spans multiple sources?','Sometimes one page answers a question; sometimes you must follow references across a filing cabinet.','Query rewriting clarifies search intent. Multi-query retrieval searches several phrasings. Graph RAG uses explicit entities and relationships when linked facts matter.','These techniques add complexity and cost. Begin with a measured baseline, then add them for demonstrated failure modes such as ambiguous wording or multi-hop questions.','To answer “Who approved the policy my team follows?”, a system may connect a team, policy and approval record.','Advanced retrieval always beats simple retrieval. It can introduce more irrelevant context.','rag',['Improve a measured failure.','Multi-hop questions need links.','Complexity has a cost.']],
  ['agent-tools','Tool calls and observations','How does an agent interact with the world?','The assistant can request a calculator or calendar, then read the result before deciding again.','A tool has a name, input schema and output. The model proposes a call; the application validates and executes it; the result returns as an observation.','Tool execution belongs to trusted application code. Validate arguments, use narrow permissions and record failures. The model should not pretend a tool succeeded without its result.','A travel assistant checks live train availability through an API before proposing a journey.','Describing a tool call in text performs the action. The application must actually execute it.','agent',['Schema describes inputs.','Runtime executes calls.','Observations inform the next step.']],
  ['agent-state','Memory, state and stopping','What does an agent remember between steps?','A notebook tracks what has already been tried so you do not repeat it forever.','Agent state stores the goal, prior messages, tool results and current plan. A stopping rule ends the loop when the task is done or a limit is reached.','Separate short-lived task state from persistent memory. Summaries can lose details; store important facts explicitly and verify them before reuse.','A booking assistant keeps candidate flights and rejected options during one session, then stops after a reservation is confirmed.','Longer agent loops are always smarter. They can repeat errors and increase cost.','agent',['State records progress.','Memory has different lifetimes.','Stop conditions are essential.']],
  ['agent-safety','Approval and agent safety','When should an agent pause before acting?','Reading a calendar is different from sending a payment. The second action needs a stronger gate.','Give agents the least access needed. Require user approval for high-impact actions, and check tool inputs against policy before execution.','Treat retrieved text and tool output as untrusted. Use allowlisted operations, audit logs, idempotency where possible and rollback plans for reversible changes.','A scheduling agent can propose an invitation but asks before emailing participants.','A system prompt alone is a sufficient permission system. Enforce permissions in code.','agent',['Access should be narrow.','Approve consequential actions.','Enforce rules outside the model.']],
  ['mcp','What is MCP?','How can one AI application discover many tools?','A common socket lets appliances connect without a custom wire for every combination.','Model Context Protocol defines a common way for AI clients and servers to exchange tools, resources and prompts.','MCP standardizes discovery and calls; it does not make a tool trustworthy or authorize it automatically. Hosts still govern permissions and validate data.','An IDE client can connect to a project tool server and expose selected repository operations to an assistant.','MCP itself is the model or the agent. It is a protocol between components.','agent',['MCP standardizes connections.','Hosts control access.','Tool output still needs scrutiny.']],
  ['framework-api','Model APIs and application boundaries','What does an AI API give you?','A service counter accepts a well-formed request and returns a result; your app still decides what to ask and how to use it.','A model API provides remote inference. Your application handles input validation, user context, retries, costs, output checks and presentation.','Use timeouts, structured errors and request IDs. Keep secrets on the server, never in browser code. Design for provider changes where practical.','A support app receives a ticket, retrieves policy, calls a model and validates its reply before display.','Calling an API is the whole product. Product behavior lives around the call.','request',['API serves inference.','App owns workflow.','Protect credentials.']],
  ['framework-orchestration','What orchestration frameworks solve','When are LangChain or LangGraph useful?','A reusable wiring harness helps when many components must pass data to each other.','Orchestration frameworks provide building blocks for model calls, prompts, tools, retrieval and stateful workflows.','Choose by actual requirements: simple code may be clearer for a short pipeline, while durable graphs help complex branching and recovery. Inspect abstractions and versioned APIs before adopting them.','A document assistant with retrieval, review and approval stages may benefit from an explicit workflow graph.','A framework makes an unreliable design reliable automatically. It organizes code; it cannot replace evaluation.','agent',['Frameworks organize repeated patterns.','Choose by workflow complexity.','Measure behavior independently.']],
  ['framework-local','Local versus hosted models','When should a team run the model itself?','Renting a kitchen versus owning one changes control, setup and ongoing work.','Hosted APIs reduce operations burden. Local serving offers more control over deployment and data flow but requires hardware and maintenance.','Compare quality, latency, throughput, privacy needs, licenses and total cost. A model that fits in memory may still fail service-level targets under concurrency.','A private document processor may serve an open-weight model inside its own network after benchmarking.','Local models are always free. Compute, engineering and reliability have real costs.','request',['Hosting is a tradeoff.','Benchmark real workloads.','Include operations cost.']],
  ['framework-pytorch','Why learn PyTorch?','What sits below high-level AI frameworks?','A workshop lets you build and inspect the machinery instead of only using a finished appliance.','PyTorch represents arrays as tensors, builds neural network modules and computes gradients with autograd.','Learning tensors, shapes, forward passes and optimizer steps helps debug training and understand model internals, even when a production app calls a hosted API.','A researcher prototypes a small classifier before deciding whether a larger pretrained model is needed.','Every AI engineer must train a giant model. Small experiments are enough to learn the mechanics.','toggle',['Tensors hold numbers.','Autograd computes gradients.','Modules organize layers.']],
  ['prod-api','Designing an AI API','What should the boundary between browser and model look like?','A restaurant menu hides kitchen details but makes ordering predictable.','An API defines accepted inputs, outputs and error behavior. Validate requests and keep model-provider credentials on the server.','Use explicit schemas, authentication, request IDs, limits and versioning. Decide whether a request is synchronous, streamed or queued based on duration and user expectations.','A /summaries endpoint accepts a document ID and returns a status plus cited summary.','An HTTP endpoint alone makes an AI system production-ready. The surrounding controls matter.','request',['Define contracts.','Validate at boundaries.','Keep secrets server-side.']],
  ['prod-streaming','Streaming, queues and async work','Why does one task return gradually while another waits in a queue?','A waiter can bring each dish when ready; a long catering order may need a ticket and pickup time.','Streaming sends partial output as it appears. A queue holds longer jobs for workers to process asynchronously.','Use cancellation, backpressure and timeouts. Keep request handlers responsive; store job state so a retry does not accidentally duplicate a costly action.','Chat tokens stream to the browser, while a thousand-page indexing job runs in a background worker.','Streaming makes the model compute faster. It mainly improves perceived latency.','request',['Stream incremental results.','Queue long jobs.','Handle cancellation and retries.']],
  ['prod-reliability','Caching, rate limits and secrets','How do you protect a busy AI service?','A library limits crowding, keeps expensive books safe and reuses prepared copies when appropriate.','Rate limits bound usage. Caches reuse valid results. Secrets such as API keys stay in protected server configuration.','Cache keys must include all inputs that affect output and respect user permissions. Use quotas, circuit breakers and clear expiry rules; do not cache sensitive content across users.','A company knowledge bot caches public FAQ answers but keeps employee-specific answers isolated.','Caching any AI answer is safe. Stale or cross-user data can be harmful.','request',['Bound usage.','Scope cache correctly.','Protect credentials.']],
  ['prod-evals','Testing and observability','How do you know an AI system is working after launch?','A dashboard tells you both how fast a car moves and whether it is going the right way.','Tests check expected behavior before release. Logs, traces and metrics reveal failures, latency and cost in production.','Track prompt and model versions, retrieval steps, tool calls, user feedback and task outcomes while minimizing sensitive logging. Alert on regressions, not just server uptime.','A support bot may be online while citation accuracy drops after a document update; quality monitoring catches it.','HTTP 200 means the AI answer was good. System health and answer quality are separate.','data',['Test with real cases.','Trace every stage.','Monitor quality and cost.']],
  ['prod-operations','Deployment, scaling and costs','What changes when a prototype gets many users?','One cashier may serve a quiet store; rush hour needs scheduling, capacity and a budget.','Deployment packages and runs the service. Scaling adds capacity as load rises. Cost depends on model calls, token volume, storage and infrastructure.','Measure p50/p95 latency, error rates, concurrency and spend per successful task. Containers aid reproducibility; orchestration is useful only when the operational need justifies it.','A document service batches ingestion, scales workers during uploads and limits expensive requests per account.','Adding more servers automatically fixes model latency. Bottlenecks may be in providers, retrieval or GPU memory.','request',['Measure bottlenecks.','Scale the right component.','Budget per successful task.']],
  ['inf-gpu','GPU memory and compute','Why does serving a model need so much memory?','A workshop needs space for the tools, unfinished work and temporary materials.','A GPU stores model weights, KV cache and temporary activations while executing many parallel calculations.','VRAM capacity limits what fits; memory bandwidth limits data movement; compute throughput limits arithmetic. GPU workloads can be memory-bound or compute-bound depending on phase and shape.','A long-context chat server may run out of KV-cache space even though weights already fit.','More GPU arithmetic alone solves every latency problem. Data movement and memory capacity also matter.','cache',['Weights occupy memory.','KV cache grows with requests.','Bandwidth and compute differ.']],
  ['inf-batching','Continuous batching and scheduling','How can a server share one GPU among many conversations?','An elevator admits new riders as others leave instead of waiting for one fixed group to finish.','Continuous batching lets a serving engine add and remove requests between decode steps. A scheduler decides who uses limited compute and KV-cache memory.','Prefill and decode have different resource profiles. Admission, chunked prefill and fairness policies affect throughput and time to first token.','A chat service serves short and long requests together while new users arrive.','A bigger batch always improves every user’s latency. Contention and queueing can make some requests slower.','request',['Requests share steps.','Scheduler manages memory.','Measure fairness and latency.']],
  ['inf-quantization','Quantization and numeric formats','How can a model use fewer bits?','A compressed photo saves space, but too much compression changes what you see.','Quantization stores or computes values with lower precision to reduce memory use and sometimes speed inference.','FP32, FP16, BF16, FP8 and integer formats have different range, precision and hardware support. Weight-only and activation quantization differ. Test task quality after conversion.','A server may fit a larger model in available VRAM using an 8-bit or 4-bit weight format.','Lower precision always means the same output and faster serving. Quality and speed depend on implementation and hardware.','cache',['Fewer bits save memory.','Formats have tradeoffs.','Benchmark quality and speed.']],
  ['inf-metrics','TTFT, TPOT and throughput','What exactly does “fast” mean for a model?','Waiting for the first word feels different from how quickly the rest of a sentence appears.','Time to first token (TTFT) measures the initial wait. Time per output token (TPOT) measures generation pace. Throughput counts work over time.','Report distributions, concurrency, prompt/output lengths and hardware. End-to-end latency includes queueing, retrieval, network and rendering, not just GPU compute.','A chat UI with quick first token but slow subsequent tokens feels different from one with a long pause then rapid output.','One tokens-per-second number describes every user experience. Workload and percentile matter.','request',['Separate initial and ongoing delay.','Measure under load.','Include full system latency.']],
  ['mm-images','Images as tensors','How does a computer see an image?','A picture becomes a grid where each cell records colour intensities.','A digital image is a tensor, often height × width × channels. Models transform those numbers into useful representations.','Preprocessing such as resizing, normalization and patching affects model input. A vision transformer treats image patches as tokens; CNNs apply local filters.','An OCR system receives pixel arrays, detects text regions and recognizes characters.','A model sees the same image a person does. It receives numerical arrays shaped by preprocessing.','data',['Pixels are numbers.','Channels encode colour.','Preprocessing matters.']],
  ['mm-vision','Vision-language models and CLIP','How can text search find an image?','Put descriptions and pictures on a shared map; related pairs land nearer each other.','Vision-language models connect visual and text representations. CLIP-style training aligns images with captions using contrastive examples.','Similarity can retrieve images by text but does not verify every claim in an image. OCR and object localization may need separate components and evaluation.','A catalog search finds pictures matching “red running shoes” from a text query.','Shared embeddings guarantee perfect visual understanding. They are learned approximations.','embedding',['Images and text can align.','Similarity supports search.','Evaluate visual failure cases.']],
  ['mm-speech','Speech recognition and synthesis','How does voice become text and back?','One system listens and transcribes; another reads a written answer aloud.','Automatic speech recognition maps audio to text. Text-to-speech turns text into audio. A voice assistant connects these stages to reasoning and actions.','Latency, accent coverage, noise, diarization and interruption handling affect quality. Streaming audio needs careful buffering and privacy controls.','A customer-service assistant transcribes a question, retrieves an answer and speaks it back.','Perfect transcript quality can be assumed. Noise and names often require correction.','request',['ASR reads speech.','TTS speaks text.','End-to-end latency matters.']],
  ['mm-generation','Diffusion and media generation','How can a model create an image?','Start with a noisy canvas, then repeatedly refine it toward a requested scene.','Diffusion models learn to reverse a noise-adding process. At generation time they iteratively denoise a latent or image representation conditioned on text or other inputs.','Sampling steps, guidance, resolution and architecture affect speed and fidelity. Generated media needs provenance and task-specific safety checks.','A design tool creates several illustration drafts from a short art direction brief.','Image generation works like next-token text generation. Common diffusion systems use a different iterative process.','decode',['Diffusion iteratively denoises.','Conditioning guides content.','Evaluate quality and provenance.']],
  ['design-chat','Design a conversational assistant','What pieces are needed beyond a chat box?','A receptionist needs a desk, records, rules for escalation and a way to remember the current conversation.','A chat product combines UI, authentication, conversation state, model calls, streaming, safety and evaluation.','Define user jobs and quality targets first. Version prompts, persist only appropriate history, measure latency and implement failure fallbacks.','An internal assistant answers policy questions and hands unresolved issues to a human.','A model endpoint and chat UI are a complete assistant. Reliability is a system property.','request',['Define jobs and quality.','Manage state and streaming.','Provide fallback paths.']],
  ['design-search','Design AI search with citations','How do you make an answer inspectable?','A researcher gives both a conclusion and the pages used to reach it.','Ingest trustworthy sources, retrieve relevant passages, synthesize an answer and link each claim to evidence.','Index freshness, source permissions, hybrid retrieval, abstention and citation verification are core requirements. Track retrieval recall and factual support separately.','A company search tool answers policy questions from versioned documents and shows exact pages.','A citation marker alone creates trust. Users need accessible supporting passages.','rag',['Source quality matters.','Cite exact passages.','Test abstention.']],
  ['design-copilot','Design a coding assistant','How can AI help without damaging a codebase?','A junior developer can propose edits, but tests and review check the result.','A coding assistant reads relevant repository context, proposes a change, runs allowed checks and shows a reviewable diff.','Repository search, tool permissions, branch isolation, test selection and human approval shape the workflow. Measure task success, regressions and time-to-review.','An assistant updates an API endpoint in a feature branch and presents passing tests and a diff.','Generated code is correct because it compiles. Behavior, security and maintainability also matter.','agent',['Read relevant context.','Make reviewable changes.','Verify with tests.']],
  ['design-rag','Design enterprise document intelligence','How do you answer from private company knowledge?','A secure library shows each person only the books they are allowed to read.','A document system ingests files, preserves access controls, retrieves permitted passages and answers with citations.','Identity-aware filtering must happen before model context assembly. Handle deletion, version updates, audit trails and sensitive logging.','A staff assistant can search HR policy but cannot retrieve another team’s confidential contract.','A vector database automatically enforces document permissions. Access rules require explicit design.','rag',['Permissions precede retrieval.','Track versions and deletion.','Audit source use.']],
  ['design-voice','Design a voice agent','What makes voice interaction different from text chat?','A conversation is turn-taking in real time: pauses, interruptions and corrections matter.','A voice agent connects audio capture, speech recognition, model reasoning, tool use and speech output with low latency.','Support interruption, partial transcripts, consent, fallback and tool confirmation. Measure end-to-end turn latency and task completion, not just model tokens per second.','A restaurant booking agent can ask a follow-up, check availability and confirm details before reserving.','Adding text-to-speech to a chatbot creates a good voice agent. Timing and interaction design are central.','request',['Voice is real-time.','Interruptions need design.','Confirm consequential actions.']],
];

const categories = {python:'python',math:'ml',ml:'ml','deep-learning':'torch',nlp:'transformers',language:'transformers',llms:'transformers',prompts:'openai','vector-search':'transformers',rag:'transformers',agents:'mcp',frameworks:'openai',production:'fastapi',inference:'inference',multimodal:'transformers','system-design':'openai',foundations:'ml'};
const trackFor = id => groups.find(g=>g.lessons.includes(id));
const extra = {
  'python-variables':{code:`age = 25  # Store an integer under the name age.\nname = "Asha"  # Store text as a string.\nis_learning = True  # Store a yes/no value as a boolean.\nprint(name, age, is_learning)  # Read the values and display them.`,codeNotes:'Change age to a quoted string. What changes about its type?',exercise:'Create a variable named tokens with value 120, then print it.'},
  'python-control':{code:`scores = [0.92, 0.31, 0.78]  # Three model confidence scores.\nfor score in scores:  # Visit each score once.\n    if score >= 0.70:  # Keep only scores meeting the threshold.\n        print("review", score)  # Show the selected score.`,exercise:'Change the threshold to 0.9. Which scores remain?'},
  'python-functions':{code:`def normalize(text: str) -> str:  # Define a reusable text-cleaning function.\n    cleaned = text.strip()  # Remove surrounding whitespace.\n    return cleaned.lower()  # Return lowercase text to the caller.\n\nquery = normalize("  Refund Policy  ")  # Call the function with input.\nprint(query)  # Display the returned value.`,exercise:'Write a function that doubles a number and returns the result.'},
  'python-collections':{code:`messages = [  # A list keeps conversation order.\n    {"role": "user", "content": "Hello"},  # A dictionary labels each field.\n    {"role": "assistant", "content": "Hi"},  # The next message stays second.\n]\nprint(messages[0]["content"])  # Read the first message's content.`,exercise:'Add a third message and print its role.'},
  'python-files':{code:`import json  # Load Python's JSON support.\nfrom pathlib import Path  # Use a portable file-path object.\n\npath = Path("settings.json")  # Point to the settings file.\ntry:  # Handle a missing or malformed file deliberately.\n    settings = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))  # Parse UTF-8 JSON.\nexcept (FileNotFoundError, json.JSONDecodeError):  # Catch expected failures.\n    settings = {}  # Continue with an empty configuration.\nprint(settings)  # Inspect the loaded result.`,exercise:'Change the fallback to a dictionary with a model key.'},
  'python-apis':{code:`import requests  # Use an HTTP client package.\n\nresponse = requests.get("https://example.com/api/status", timeout=5)  # Make a bounded request.\nresponse.raise_for_status()  # Surface non-success HTTP responses.\ndata = response.json()  # Parse the returned JSON body.\nprint(data)  # Inspect it before using its fields.`,codeNotes:'The URL is illustrative. Use a real endpoint when practicing.',exercise:'Describe how you would handle a timeout separately from an invalid JSON response.'},
  'math-vectors':{code:`a = [1, 2]  # First two-dimensional vector.\nb = [3, 4]  # Second vector with matching dimensions.\ndot = sum(x * y for x, y in zip(a, b))  # Multiply pairs and add.\nprint(dot)  # The result is 11.`,exercise:'Compute the dot product of [1, 0] and [0, 1].'},
  'math-gradients':{code:`weight = 4.0  # Start with an imperfect parameter.\nlearning_rate = 0.1  # Choose the size of each update.\nfor step in range(5):  # Perform five small optimization steps.\n    gradient = 2 * (weight - 2)  # Derivative of loss (weight - 2)^2.\n    weight -= learning_rate * gradient  # Move against the gradient.\n    print(round(weight, 3))  # Watch the parameter approach 2.`,exercise:'Try learning_rate = 1.2. What happens to the updates?'},
  'ml-metrics':{code:`tp = 8  # Eight true positives.\nfp = 2  # Two false alarms.\nfn = 4  # Four missed positives.\nprecision = tp / (tp + fp)  # Fraction of positive predictions that were right.\nrecall = tp / (tp + fn)  # Fraction of real positives found.\nprint(precision, recall)  # Print 0.8 and about 0.667.`,exercise:'If false positives double, which metric changes?'},
  'ml-splits':{code:`records = list(range(10))  # Ten time-ordered observations.\ntrain = records[:6]  # Fit on the earliest six.\nvalidation = records[6:8]  # Tune on the next two.\ntest = records[8:]  # Evaluate once on the latest two.\nprint(train, validation, test)  # Check the boundaries.`,exercise:'Why would random splitting be risky if each record is a later day?'},
  'ml-regression':{code:`area = 80  # Home area in square metres.\nprice_per_m2 = 3000  # A learned linear coefficient.\nbase_price = 20000  # A learned bias term.\nprediction = price_per_m2 * area + base_price  # Linear model output.\nprint(prediction)  # Predicted price is 260000.`,exercise:'Predict a 100-square-metre home with the same coefficients.'},
  'ml-overfitting':{code:`train_errors = [0.45, 0.28, 0.12, 0.04]  # Error falls as complexity rises.\nvalid_errors = [0.48, 0.31, 0.23, 0.39]  # Held-out error eventually rises.\nbest = min(range(len(valid_errors)), key=valid_errors.__getitem__)  # Pick by validation.\nprint(best, valid_errors[best])  # Complexity index 2 generalizes best here.`,exercise:'Why should the test set remain unused while selecting complexity?'},
  'dl-neuron':{code:`inputs = [2.0, 3.0]  # Two numeric features.\nweights = [0.5, -0.2]  # Learned influence of each feature.\nbias = 0.1  # Shift the result.\nscore = sum(x * w for x, w in zip(inputs, weights)) + bias  # Weighted sum.\noutput = max(0.0, score)  # ReLU activation removes negative values.\nprint(output)  # Inspect this neuron's output.`,exercise:'Change the second weight to +0.2. How does output change?'},
  'dl-forward':{code:`layer_one = lambda x: max(0, 2 * x - 1)  # First weighted layer with ReLU.\nlayer_two = lambda h: 0.5 * h + 0.2  # Second layer maps hidden value to output.\ninput_value = 3  # Supply one numeric feature.\nhidden = layer_one(input_value)  # Compute the intermediate representation.\noutput = layer_two(hidden)  # Feed it into the next layer.\nprint(hidden, output)  # Inspect both stages of the forward pass.`,exercise:'Set input_value to 0. What happens after ReLU?'},
  'nlp-counts':{code:`documents = ["red apple", "green apple"]  # Two short documents.\nvocabulary = sorted(set(" ".join(documents).split()))  # Unique words in order.\nfor document in documents:  # Visit each document.\n    counts = [document.split().count(word) for word in vocabulary]  # Count each word.\n    print(counts)  # This row is its bag-of-words vector.`,exercise:'Add “red pear” and rebuild the vocabulary.'},
  'nlp-tfidf':{code:`import math  # Use the logarithm for a tiny IDF example.\ndocuments = ["apple red", "apple green", "banana yellow"]  # Our corpus.\nterm = "banana"  # Term whose distinctiveness we want.\ndocument_frequency = sum(term in doc.split() for doc in documents)  # Count matching docs.\nidf = math.log((1 + len(documents)) / (1 + document_frequency)) + 1  # Smoothed IDF.\nprint(round(idf, 2))  # Rare terms get a larger weight.`,exercise:'Change term to “apple”. Which receives a lower IDF?'},
  'attention-math':{code:`import math  # Provide exponentials for softmax.\nquery = [1.0, 0.0]  # What the current token seeks.\nkeys = [[1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0]]  # What two tokens advertise.\nscores = [sum(q * k for q, k in zip(query, key)) / math.sqrt(2) for key in keys]  # Scaled dot products.\nweights = [math.exp(score) for score in scores]  # Convert scores to positive weights.\nweights = [weight / sum(weights) for weight in weights]  # Normalize to one.\nprint(weights)  # The first key receives more attention.`,exercise:'Swap the query coordinates. Which key gets more weight?'},
  'llm-sampling':{code:`import math  # Provide exponentials for a tiny softmax demo.\nlogits = [2.0, 1.0, 0.0]  # Raw model scores for three candidate tokens.\ntemperature = 1.0  # Higher values flatten the distribution.\nweights = [math.exp(value / temperature) for value in logits]  # Rescale scores.\nprobabilities = [value / sum(weights) for value in weights]  # Normalize to 1.\nprint(probabilities)  # Inspect the resulting distribution.`,exercise:'Compare temperature 0.5 and 2.0. Which is flatter?'},
  'vector-similarity':{code:`import math  # Supply square-root for vector lengths.\na = [1.0, 0.0]  # First nonzero vector.\nb = [0.8, 0.6]  # Second nonzero vector.\ndot = sum(x * y for x, y in zip(a, b))  # Find aligned direction.\nnorm_a = math.sqrt(sum(x * x for x in a))  # Length of a.\nnorm_b = math.sqrt(sum(y * y for y in b))  # Length of b.\nprint(dot / (norm_a * norm_b))  # Cosine similarity is 0.8.`,exercise:'What value do perpendicular unit vectors produce?'},
  'rag-ranking':{code:`candidates = [  # Retrieved passages with illustrative similarity scores.\n    {"text": "Refunds take five days", "score": 0.91, "source": "policy-7"},  # Best match.\n    {"text": "Shipping takes two days", "score": 0.42, "source": "policy-3"},  # Weak match.\n]\nselected = sorted(candidates, key=lambda item: item["score"], reverse=True)[:1]  # Keep top one.\nfor passage in selected:  # Preserve evidence with the chosen text.\n    print(passage["text"], passage["source"])  # Show text and citation ID.`,exercise:'What would change if the highest-scoring passage were outdated?'},
  'agent-tools':{code:`def calculator(a: float, b: float) -> float:  # Define a narrow allowed tool.\n    return a + b  # Perform the requested arithmetic.\n\nrequest = {"a": 2, "b": 3}  # Model-proposed arguments after validation.\nresult = calculator(**request)  # Application code executes the tool.\nobservation = {"tool": "calculator", "result": result}  # Record the result.\nprint(observation)  # Feed this observation into the next decision.`,exercise:'What should happen if the proposed arguments contain text instead of numbers?'},
  'prod-api':{code:`from fastapi import FastAPI  # Import the web application class.\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel  # Import request validation support.\n\napp = FastAPI()  # Create the API application.\nclass Question(BaseModel):  # Define the input shape.\n    text: str  # Require a text field.\n\n@app.post("/questions")  # Register an HTTP POST route.\ndef ask(question: Question):  # Receive a validated request.\n    return {"received": question.text}  # Return a structured response.`,exercise:'Add a max-length validation rule for the input text.'},
  'prod-evals':{code:`cases = [  # Tiny illustrative evaluation set.\n    {"question": "Refund time?", "expected": "five days"},  # Known target.\n    {"question": "Support hours?", "expected": "9 to 5"},  # Another target.\n]\nanswers = ["five days", "24 hours"]  # Results from a candidate system.\ncorrect = sum(answer == case["expected"] for case, answer in zip(cases, answers))  # Count exact matches.\nprint(correct / len(cases))  # Show 0.5 on this toy metric.`,codeNotes:'Production evaluation needs more cases and richer correctness checks than exact string matching.',exercise:'Add an unanswerable case. What should a safe answer look like?'},
  'inf-metrics':{code:`started = 0.0  # Example request start in seconds.\nfirst_token = 0.42  # First output token arrived at 0.42 seconds.\nfinished = 1.62  # Final output token arrived at 1.62 seconds.\noutput_tokens = 61  # Total number of generated tokens.\nttft = first_token - started  # Initial waiting time.\ntpot = (finished - first_token) / (output_tokens - 1)  # Gap per later token.\nprint(ttft, tpot)  # Print 0.42 s and 0.02 s/token.`,exercise:'Double the first-token time while keeping decode speed. Which metric changes?'},
};

